1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,033
Greenland looks enormous on many Mercator maps. But Africa has about fourteen times its reported land area. The map and the land are measuring different things.

2
00:00:11,033 --> 00:00:22,633
On a sphere, meridians converge toward the poles. Mercator keeps them equally spaced on the page. The same longitude gap is therefore stretched more at high latitude.

3
00:00:22,633 --> 00:00:35,133
To preserve local angles, Mercator stretches north-south distances by the same local factor. Near sixty degrees, a tiny patch is doubled in both directions, giving four times the area.

4
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:45,033
That is a local result, not one multiplier for all of Greenland. The country spans different latitudes, and each part is magnified differently.

5
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:58,333
Now rotate the actual generalized boundary on a sphere toward the equator. Its land area stays the same, but its footprint shrinks on this fixed-scale Mercator map. This is not a two-dimensional resize.

6
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:08,567
Mercator makes constant-heading routes straight, though they are usually not the shortest routes. Its poles project to infinity, so a finite map must cut them off.

7
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:20,800
For comparing areas, an equal-area map such as Equal Earth is useful. It preserves area proportions, not every shape or angle. The right projection depends on the question.
